## 4. Validate, deploy, run one task first

```
VARS='--var=catalog=<catalog> --var=schema=<schema> --var=volume=<volume> --var=node_type_id=<node type>'
databricks bundle validate $$VARS
databricks bundle deploy $$VARS
databricks bundle run ${job_key} --only ${first_task} $$VARS      # one task first
databricks bundle run ${job_key} $$VARS                            # then the whole job
```

Tasks (one per pipeline step, each a `python_wheel_task` of the same wheel):

| Task | Depends on |
|---|---|
${tasks_table}

## 5. After the run

1. Compare the wheel in the job run's library list with `target-manifest.json` (`hashes.wheel`): the wheel built
   and dry-run here is `dist/${wheel_file}`, sha256 `${wheel_sha256}`. A different hash means other code ran.
2. Download `work/` and `outputs/` from the Volume. Checking them against the legacy reference
   (`/migrate verify --against-target <dir>`) is planned after the demo; until then the job's outputs are unverified.

## 6. What has not been tested

- Nothing was deployed and no Databricks workspace was contacted; the code has not run on a Databricks cluster.
- Unity Catalog permissions, cluster policies, node availability and PyPI reachability from the cluster.
- Volume (FUSE) behaviour beyond the seek emulation of the dry run (latency, consistency); random reads of input
  rasters on the Volume are allowed and assumed to work.
- The runtime's real package versions (only the declared ones were used), Spark, and the runtime's notebook/IPython
  hooks.
- That `${spark_version}` is the exact runtime key in your workspace (check with your own CLI).
- Performance and memory at full scale (the dry-run peak memory per task is in `target-check.json`, T11).
